<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.2: Linear Function Constant Rate of Change</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01517</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.2: Linear Function Constant Rate of Change</md:title>
    <md:uuid>49621e04-9a18-49b3-9f76-cc14e354998d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here is a graph of \(y=f(x)\) where \(f(x)=2x+5\).</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <figure id="fig-00001">
          <media alt="Graph of an increasing linear function, x y plane, origin O.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_17_03_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003"> How do the values of \(f\) change whenever \(x\) increases by 1, for instance, when it increases from 1 to 2, or from 19 to 20? Be prepared to explain or show how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004"/>
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">The value of \(f\) increases by 2 whenever the input increases by 1. For example:</para>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
            <item> From the graph, we can see the \(y\)-value increasing by 2 when the \(x\)-value increases by 1.</item>
            <item> \(f(1)=7\) and \(f(2)=9\). The difference between \(f(1)\) and \(f(2)\) is 2. Likewise, \(f(20)=45\) and \(f(19)=43\), and \(f(20)−f(19)=2\).</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"> Here is an expression we can use to find the difference in the values of \(f\) when the input changes from \(x\) to \(x+1\).</para>
          <para id="para-00008">
            <quote id="quote-00001">\(\lbrack 2(x+1)+5 \rbrack − \lbrack 2x+5 \rbrack \)</quote>
            <quote id="quote-00002">Does this expression have the same value as what you found in the previous questions? Show your reasoning.</quote>
         </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009"/>
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Yes. \(2(x+1)=2x+2\). Replacing \(2(x+1)\) with \(2x+2\) and performing arithmetic gives \(f(x+1)−f(x)=2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012"> How do the values of \(f\) change whenever \(x\) increases by 4? Explain or show how you know.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013"/>
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015"> The values of \(f\) change by 8 if the input is increased by 4. Increasing the input by 4 is the same as increasing the input by 1 four successive times, and each time the value of \(f\) increased by 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00016"> Write an expression that shows the change in the values of \(f\) when the input value changes from \(x\) to \(x+4\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017"/>
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00019"> \(f(x+4)−f(x)\) can be expressed as \(\lbrack 2(x+4)+5 \rbrack− \lbrack 2x+5 \rbrack \).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00020"> Show or explain how that expression has a value of 8.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021"/>
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Applying the distributive property to \( \lbrack 2(x+4)+5 \rbrack − \lbrack 2x+5 \rbrack \) gives us: \(2x+8+5−2x−5\) or \(2x−2x+8+5−5\), which equals 8.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>